    "available_at": "2026-11-17T22:00:48.198809+00:00",
    "body": "A decision handed down by the U.S. Supreme Court Monday is setting up the possibility of a major decision on the question of shipper and recipient liability in a post-Montgomery world.\nThe high court granted certiorari in the case of Casarez vs. Irigoyen Farms et. al. in an action known as a Grant, Vacate, Remand Order (GVR). Unlike a more traditional certiorari finding, a granting of GVR allows the high court to send the case back to a lower court without actually ruling on its merits, but keeping open a chance the nine justices will take up the issue later.\nWith its GVR order, the case of Casarez vs. Irigoyen Farms will be returned to a California appellate court that will take up anew the question of whether a shipper or even their customer can be hit with liability coming out of a crash. The legal process in Casarez so far has firmly answered “no” to that question.\nBut that was before Montgomery vs. Caribe Transport II stripped away certain protections for brokers, with the possibility that the new landscape for litigation could eventually create liability for shippers–and their customers–as well.\nHad that SCOTUS certiorari grant and accompanying remand order not been issued, the case would have effectively shut down in the Golden State, with a body of decisions favorable to the shipper and recipient formulated before Montgomery turned the freight world upside down.\nBiggest retailer is in the case\nOne of those defendants in the et. al list: Walmart (NYSE: WMT). Irigoyen was sending produce from its operations in Selma, California to a Walmart distribution center in Nevada.\nIrigoyen and Walmart, in earlier decisions, were ruled to not have liability in the September 2019 crash that killed Olivia Mendoza when a truck driven by independent contractor Andre Hill, carrying produce from Irigoyen to Walmart, went through a stop sign and slammed into Mendoza’s car near Fresno. She died at the scene.\nPlaintiff Casarez was Mendoza’s mother.\nHill was ultimately sentenced to two years in prison after being found guilty on a charge of vehicular manslaughter.\nAccording to briefs filed in the case, Irigoyen had contracted with Royal Violet Logistics to arrange for the delivery of the produce. Royal Violet then contracted with SIO Logistics, who then hired Gold Coast Logistics as carrier of the load. Gold Coast hired Hill to move the freight to Walmart. .\nIt was not immediately clear what the outcome was of any litigation directed at the 3Pls or the carrier.\nBut their presence is largely irrelevant to the more significant aspect of the case. Casarez vs. Irigoyen could be groundbreaking in the ongoing post-Montgomery reckoning that will need to shake out whether the expanded legal landscape against brokers created by Montgomery might expand to include shippers–in this case Irigoyen–or even freight customers, in this case Walmart.\nWinning in the lower courts\nIrigoyen and Walmart, in earlier decisions in the California court system, were ruled to not have liability in the crash.\nFirst stop for the Casarez case was Fresno County Superior Court, which in September 2023 granted summary judgment to Irigoyen and Walmart and dismissed them from the lawsuit. Their pleading was that the Federal Aviation Administration Authorization Act and its prohibition of a state action that could impact a “price, route or service” preempted action against the two companies.\nThe two defendants also argued the F4A “safety exception” that would allow torts or other action in the event of an incident like a crash could not suck them in, because Walmart and Irigoyen were not “motor vehicles.” The safety exception’s wording is that it is “with respect to motor vehicles.”\nThe decision in Montgomery vs. Caribe Transport II ended any debate over whether the actions of brokers could be viewed under F4A as being “with respect to motor vehicles.” They can be, and in the years to come, they almost certainly will be.\nNext came the Fifth Appellate District of the California Court of Appeal which in September 2025 affirmed the Fresno County court decision. Soon after, in January 2026, the California Supreme Court denied review.\nA whole new world\nBut then Montgomery vs. Caribe Transport II landed in May , undercutting much of the rationale for the findings of the two California courts. That led Casarez to petition the U.S. Supreme Court for certiorari, which was granted Monday.\nA raging debate in the industry now is whether “with respect to motor vehicles” can also be used to bring a shipper or freight recipient into a lawsuit over a crash or other incident.\nCasarez vs. Irigoyen Farms could be the platform for settling that question, even as shippers so far have prevailed since the ruling in Montgomery. For example, just days after the Montgomery decision, a Texas court rejected liability for Home Depot (NYSE: HD) in a fatal wreck where its products were being hauled by Werner Enterprises (NASDAQ: WERN) .\nWalmart released a brief statement on the decision. “(Monday’s) order grants the relief Walmart asked for in its response to Ms. Casarez’s petition,” the company said. “We will continue to defend the company in the California Court of Appeal.”\nIn its brief to the Supreme Court, Walmart tried to separate itself out from the process that led Hill to be hired. “As the intended recipient of the shipment, Walmart played no role in arranging for transportation,” the company said. “Walmart did not select the broker, the motor carrier, or the driver. It did not provide or maintain the vehicle. Nor did it participate in any other way.”\nAttorney Gregory Reed, a partner with the law firm of Hanson Bridgett and leader of its Transportation and Logistics practice group, wrote about the case last year after the appellate court ruling but before the California Supreme Court declined review and before Montgomery.\nCase is one to watch\nIn an email to FreightWaves, Reed said the Walmart/Irigoyen case is “the most important shipper-receive liability case to watch right now.”\nBut he added that “it is unclear whether the door is open for a monumental ruling,” as occurred with Montgomery.\nReed noted that although the Supreme Court’s action keeps the case alive, for now it had “passed on taking up the question that both Irigoyen Farm and (Casarez) requested: whether F4A preemption can be invoked by parties, such as shippers and receivers further up the supply chain, other than brokers and motor carriers as expressly enumerated in the statute.”\nAs far as whether the certiorari grant might pave the way for later Supreme Court review, Reed said there is no circuit court split at this point on the question of shipper/receiver liability that might spur such a review.\nAnd that lack of a circuit court consensus is often necessary for any issue to be reviewed by the nine justices. For example, after earlier certiorari rejections by the Supreme Court on the issue of broker liability, the court finally granted review in Montgomery when those split decisions began to pile up.\nSecond, Reed noted a distinction between Walmart and Irigoyen: the latter had driver Hill onsite where multiple people said he was visibly fatigued. That could be an issue for the produce provider, but it isn’t for Walmart.\nMore articles by John Kingston\nAmazon-focused bill likely to pass in NYC but only after changes\nAmazon workers’ union gets a contract, but it’s in Canada\nTransportation committee in House set for overhaul post-election",
